{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>And the man said, The woman, etc.</b> One needs to know what was in his language in answering ״The woman,״ etc. — even the lightest of the light would not give such an answer, and certainly not to a great king. Furthermore, his saying <b>from the tree</b> was unnecessary; he needed to say only ״she gave me and I ate,״ and it is self-evident that he is speaking of the tree mentioned. And so we find that Eve said in her answer, ״The serpent deceived me and I ate,״ and she had no need to say ״from the tree.״" "<b>And it appears</b> that the man's intention was that he knew nothing, except that the woman brought before him the prepared food and he ate, and he is not obligated to inquire about what is placed before him — for from where? For is not the entire earth before him, full of delicacies that HaShem planted? And he was precise in saying <b>whom You gave with me</b>, so as not to obligate him to search and scrutinize after her to know what is brought before him, since this woman — HaShem gave her to be with him as a help and to benefit, and no evil descends from heaven, and he has no need to examine after her, for presumably her deeds are seemly. And you will find that they of blessed memory said (Kiddushin 76a): ״We do not examine from the altar and upward,״ etc., end quote. This means that whoever is established in a holy place has a complete presumption of fitness upon him, and all the more so a great woman such as this, whom HaShem created for him as a help — he need not search after her regarding what she places before him." "And his saying <b>from the tree</b> means: from a tree planted in the garden, for the whole of it is filled with HaShem's plantings (Genesis 2:8–9). And it is precise, his saying <b>from the tree</b>, for it is not of the tree mentioned in the words of HaShem that he speaks, but rather of a tree from among the trees of the garden. And when we say that it is of the Tree of Knowledge that he speaks, the intention is not that he knew that from it she brought him and he ate, but rather after he ate he knew that from it he had eaten, by means of his perception through feeling the change; but at the time of the act he knew nothing and asked nothing, on account of his trust in the woman that HaShem had given with him — and this is what he claimed in saying ״whom You gave with me,״ etc. Furthermore, it is possible to say that the man too erred, for HaShem forbade only the fruit and not the tree, and it is his saying <b>from the tree</b>; but this is not a correct interpretation for several reasons, the first being the principal one." "<b>And now</b> there is in the hand of man no sin except unwitting, and it is possible that he has the status of one compelled, for there was no room to say to him, you should have taken heed not to sin, and he did not suspect the woman that she would feed him something forbidden, since she was given to him by El Elyon. And therefore you will find that HaShem did not curse the man but rather the earth. The truth is that his error was that he did not guard himself in his words to Eve and did not mention to her except the fruit, as is heard from her words to the serpent, for from this the sin was drawn to Eve, as we explained (Genesis 3:3)."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {2 "The last clause — וראשון עיקר — is taken to mean 'and the first [reason] is the principal one,' though the terse formulation leaves some ambiguity."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:38:21.971732685Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}